==English==
[[File:2013-01-02 00-00-55-Waning-gibbous-moon.jpg|thumb|A [[gibbous moon]] (sense 2)]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|gibbous}}, from {{der|en|la|gibbus|t=humped, hunched}}, probably cognate with {{m|la|cubō|t=bend oneself, lie down}}, {{cog|it|gobba|t=humpback}}, {{cog|el|κύφος|t=humpback, bent}}, {{m|el|κύβος|t=cube, vertebra}}, {{cog|es|giboso|t=humped}}. Also ultimately compare dialectal {{cog|no|keiv||slanted, wrong}}, {{cog|de|schief||crooked, slanting}} and {{cog|nl|scheef||crooked, slanting}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɡɪbəs/}}; {{q|uncommon, nonstandard}} {{IPA|en|/ˈd͡ʒɪbəs/}}<ref>{{R:MWO}}</ref>
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-gibbous.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪbəs|s=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Curved or [[bulge]]d outward.
#* {{RQ:Hardy Mayor of Casterbridge|chapter=[[s:The Mayor of Casterbridge/Chapter 22|chapter 22]]|passage=In fact, what these '''gibbous''' human shapes specially represented was ready money—money insistently ready [...]}}
# {{lb|en|astronomy|of a celestial body}} Having more than half (but not the whole) of its [[disc]] [[illuminate]]d.
#: {{cot|en|crescent}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2021|author=w:Ruth Ozeki|title=w:The Book of Form and Emptiness|publisher=Canongate Books (2022)|page=252|passage=On December 7, 1972, the Apollo 17 astronauts took a [[w:The Blue Marble|photograph]] of a '''gibbous''' Earth at a distance of eighteen thousand miles from its surface.}}
# [[humpback|Humpbacked]].
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|book=8|passage=A pointed flinty rock, all bare and black,<br>Grew '''gibbous''' from behind the mountain's back;}}

